// Independently authored fictional exercises. Medium adds linked calculations;
// Hard adds uncertainty, competing constraints, or a review decision.
export type LevelChallenge={focus:string;learn:string;example:string;prompt:string;answer:number;unit:string;hint:string;question:string;choices:string[];correct:number;explain:string};
const C=(focus:string,learn:string,example:string,prompt:string,answer:number,unit:string,hint:string,question:string,right:string,wrong:string,other:string,explain:string):LevelChallenge=>({focus,learn,example,prompt,answer,unit,hint,question,choices:[wrong,right,other],correct:1,explain});
export const LEVEL_CHALLENGES:Record<string,[LevelChallenge,LevelChallenge]>={
 'probability-and-expected-outcomes':[
 C('Include the cost of participating','An expected payoff is not expected profit. Subtract any cost paid in every outcome after weighting the payoffs.','A 50% chance of receiving $20 gives $10 expected payoff; a $6 entry cost leaves $4 expected profit.','A 40% chance of $30 and 60% chance of $0 costs $7 to enter. What is expected net profit?',5,'dollars','0.4 × 30 + 0.6 × 0 − 7.','Which cost belongs in expected net profit?','The entry cost in every outcome','Only costs in winning outcomes','No cost if expected payoff is positive','A fee paid regardless of the result reduces the expectation in every scenario.'),
 C('Find the break-even probability','When a decision has a win and a loss, solve for the success probability where expected net profit equals zero. Estimated probabilities can be wrong.','With +$30 on success and −$10 otherwise, 30p − 10(1−p)=0 gives p=25%.','A strategy gains $80 on success and loses $20 otherwise, before a $5 cost each attempt. What success probability makes expected net profit zero?',25,'percent','80p − 20(1−p) − 5 = 0. Solve 100p = 25.','Your estimated probability is slightly above break-even. What should you review?','Estimation uncertainty and downside capacity','Only the positive expected value','A guarantee that the next attempt wins','A fragile estimated edge can disappear after costs or probability error.')],
 'returns-and-descriptive-statistics':[
 C('Separate price return from total return','Cash distributions belong in total return. Use the same starting value and holding period when comparing investments.','A $50 share ends at $52 and pays $1: (52+1−50)/50 = 6%.','A share starts at $80, ends at $84, and pays a $2 distribution. What is total return?',7.5,'percent','(84 + 2 − 80) / 80 × 100.','Which comparison is consistent?','Compare total returns over the same period','Compare a price level with a return','Ignore distributions for only one investment','Cash distributions and consistent periods prevent a misleading comparison.'),
 C('Distinguish average return from wealth growth','An arithmetic average can be zero while compounded wealth falls. Volatility makes the path of returns matter.','A 25% rise followed by a 20% fall returns 100 to 100, despite a positive arithmetic average.','A portfolio starts at 100, gains 50%, then loses 40%. What is its cumulative return?',-10,'percent','100 × 1.5 × 0.6 = 90. Compare 90 with 100.','Why does the arithmetic average mislead here?','It does not multiply the period growth factors','It always equals compounded return','It excludes the initial price by mistake','Wealth compounds through successive changes in the investment base.')],
 'python-and-data-fundamentals':[
 C('Validate before transforming','Duplicate timestamps and missing prices need explicit handling before creating returns. Dropping a row changes which dates become adjacent.','Five valid consecutive prices produce four one-period returns.','A daily dataset contains 8 rows, including 2 exact duplicates. After removing them, 6 valid consecutive prices remain. How many daily returns can you calculate?',5,'returns','Six ordered prices give six minus one adjacent changes.','A missing date appears between two observations. What must you check?','Whether their return spans more than one period','Treat the interval as daily automatically','Replace the missing price with zero','A valid row count alone does not establish the time interval.'),
 C('Audit joins and data leakage','Joining market data to company information requires using the date that information became available, not just the financial period it describes.','A statement for March published in May was unavailable to an April signal.','A join matches each of 120 price rows to two duplicate company rows. How many extra rows are created beyond the intended 120?',120,'rows','120 × 2 − 120.','How should a historical signal use publication dates?','Join to information available by the decision time','Use the latest revised data for every past date','Match only the calendar year','Point-in-time joins and uniqueness checks address different data risks.')],
 'time-series-and-financial-mathematics':[
 C('Compound and discount consistently','Compounding moves value forward; discounting reverses it. Match the rate to the number and length of periods.','110 received in one year at a 10% discount rate has present value 100.','What is the present value of 121 received in two years at 10% per year?',100,'dollars','121 / 1.1².','When can an annual rate be used directly?','For periods measured consistently in years','For any monthly calculation without conversion','Only if the cash flow is guaranteed','Rate and time units must agree; a discount calculation alone does not remove uncertainty.'),
 C('Challenge the information timeline','A correct compounding formula cannot repair a backtest that uses future information. Separate the signal time, execution time, and evaluation period.','An end-of-day signal normally cannot assume an earlier execution price without a supported execution model.','A balance of 1,000 gains 20%, loses 10%, then incurs a $30 cost. What is the ending balance?',1050,'dollars','1,000 × 1.20 × 0.90 − 30.','A signal uses today’s closing price but claims execution before that close. What is the concern?','The assumed execution may use information not yet available','The number of periods is always wrong','Compounding is unnecessary','Document a feasible timing rule before interpreting strategy performance.')],
 'portfolio-allocation-and-diversification':[
 C('Rebalance after prices move','Weights change when assets earn different returns. Rebalancing trades back toward a chosen allocation and may create costs.','Two $50 positions become $60 and $50; the first weight is 60/110, not 50%.','A 1,000 portfolio starts 60% in A and 40% in B. A gains 10%; B is flat. What is total ending value?',1060,'dollars','600 × 1.10 + 400.','Why did A’s weight rise?','Its value grew while B’s value stayed flat','The original target changed automatically','Diversification guarantees rising prices','Actual weights drift even when a policy target remains fixed.'),
 C('Stress concentration and correlation','The two-asset variance formula includes a covariance term. Low historical correlation may not persist in a stress event.','At perfect positive correlation, equal weights in assets with 10% and 20% volatility give 15% portfolio volatility.','Two equally weighted assets each have 20% volatility and correlation 0.5. What is portfolio variance as a decimal?',0.03,'variance','0.5² × 0.2² + 0.5² × 0.2² + 2 × 0.5 × 0.5 × 0.2 × 0.2 × 0.5.','What should accompany a low estimated portfolio risk?','Correlation stress tests and concentration review','A promise that losses cannot occur','Removal of all individual exposure checks','Covariance estimates are sample-dependent and can change when conditions worsen.')],
 'backtesting-and-transaction-costs':[
 C('Translate basis points into cash costs','One basis point is 0.01%. Charge the stated cost to the traded amount and count the sides actually traded.','Ten basis points on $20,000 costs $20 for one side.','A round trip trades $50,000 on entry and $50,000 on exit at 10 basis points each side. Gross profit is $800. What is net profit?',700,'dollars','Each side costs 50,000 × 0.001 = 50. Subtract both sides.','Which turnover matters for modeled costs?','The total amount actually traded across relevant sides','Only the number of winning trades','Only ending holdings','A cost model needs explicit turnover, rates, and execution assumptions.'),
 C('Evaluate an out-of-sample result','A separate evaluation sample should stay separate while choosing the rule. Slippage and higher costs can erase a small apparent edge.','A 1.2% gross result less 0.4% commissions and 0.5% slippage leaves approximately 0.3%.','A $100,000 test makes $1,500 gross. Commission costs $400 and stressed slippage costs $1,300. What is net profit?',-200,'dollars','1,500 − 400 − 1,300.','What if you retune after every look at the test sample?','The sample is no longer an independent evaluation','Independence automatically improves','Costs become irrelevant','Repeated feedback from the test set turns it into part of model selection.')],
 'derivatives-and-black-scholes':[
 C('Separate option payoff from profit','A bought option’s expiry profit subtracts the premium paid from its payoff. Contract multipliers convert per-unit amounts into contract totals.','A call pays 12 per share and cost 5 per share: profit is 7 per share before fees.','A call has strike 100, expiry spot 112, and premium 5 per share. One contract covers 100 shares. What is expiry profit before fees?',700,'dollars','(max(112 − 100, 0) − 5) × 100.','A positive payoff guarantees a profitable purchase.','False; compare payoff with premium and costs','True for every call','True whenever spot is above strike','An option can finish in the money while failing to recover its purchase cost.'),
 C('Check put-call parity assumptions','For European options on a non-dividend-paying asset, C − P = S − present value of the strike under the standard model. Contract terms must match.','If spot is 100 and present value of strike is 95, a call at 8 implies a put at 3.','Matching European options have spot 105, present value of strike 98, and call value 12. What put value is consistent with parity?',5,'dollars','P = C − S + PV(strike) = 12 − 105 + 98.','A quoted pair violates your calculation. What comes first?','Check dividends, exercise style, timing, quotes, and costs','Declare a guaranteed tradable profit immediately','Assume all option contracts are identical','Parity comparisons depend on aligned contracts, inputs, and trading assumptions.')],
 'risk-modeling-and-drawdown':[
 C('Measure the recovery needed after a loss','A loss reduces the base from which recovery grows. A 20% loss needs a 25% gain to return to the original peak.','100 falls to 80; 20/80 = 25% recovery.','A portfolio falls from 200 to 150. What percentage gain is required to recover to 200?',100/3,'percent','(200 − 150) / 150 × 100. Round to two decimals.','Why is recovery larger than the loss percentage?','The gain applies to a smaller starting balance','Loss percentages always equal recovery percentages','The original peak changes automatically','The denominators differ: loss uses the peak, recovery uses the trough.'),
 C('Trace the running high-water mark','Maximum drawdown compares each value with the highest earlier value. A later recovery does not erase the historical decline.','A path 100, 120, 90, 110 has a maximum drawdown of 25%.','A wealth path is 100, 130, 104, 140, 98. What is its maximum drawdown?',30,'percent','The largest decline is from 140 to 98: 42/140 × 100.','Why add stress scenarios to historical drawdown?','The observed sample may omit severe but plausible events','Historical drawdown forecasts the exact next loss','Stress testing removes all model risk','A sample statistic describes observed history, not the full set of possible futures.')],
 'quantitative-research-and-model-review':[
 C('Keep a research record','Record all tested variants, not just the winner. An appealing result after many trials can reflect selection rather than a repeatable effect.','Testing 20 variants and reporting only 1 hides 19 unsuccessful attempts.','A team tests 40 variants and publishes 4. What percentage of tested variants were not published?',90,'percent','(40 − 4) / 40 × 100.','What should a research log preserve?','Rules, data versions, costs, and unsuccessful variants','Only the highest return','Only the final chart colors','A complete record makes the selection process reproducible and reviewable.'),
 C('Quantify the multiple-testing concern','With independent tests under null hypotheses, the chance of at least one false positive is 1 minus the chance of none. Real tests may be dependent.','Two independent tests at 5% false-positive probability each give 1 − 0.95² = 9.75% chance of at least one.','For 3 independent null tests with 5% false-positive probability each, what is the chance of at least one false positive?',14.2625,'percent','(1 − 0.95³) × 100. Round to two decimals.','Does one significant result among many establish an edge?','No; account for selection and seek independent evidence','Yes; the other tests do not matter','Yes; significance guarantees economic profit','Statistical selection and economic performance are distinct review questions.')],
 'accounting-equation-and-transactions':[
 C('Track multiple effects','Borrowing increases both cash and liabilities. Paying an expense reduces cash and equity through income; it does not repay debt.','Starting assets 10,000, liabilities 4,000, equity 6,000; a 1,000 expense reduces assets and equity by 1,000.','Assets start at 15,000 and liabilities at 4,000. The business borrows 2,000 and pays 1,500 rent. What is ending equity?',9500,'dollars','Initial equity = 11,000. Borrowing leaves it unchanged; rent reduces it by 1,500.','Which event reduces equity in this example?','Recognizing rent expense','Borrowing principal','Moving cash between bank accounts','Financing and expense recognition have different effects on the equation.'),
 C('Reconcile an equity roll-forward','Ending equity connects opening equity with income, owner contributions, and distributions. Check the same reporting period throughout.','Equity 20,000 + income 3,000 + contributions 2,000 − distributions 1,000 = 24,000.','Opening equity is 40,000. Net income is 8,000, owners contribute 5,000 and receive 3,000. Closing liabilities are 22,000. What are closing assets?',72000,'dollars','Closing equity = 40,000 + 8,000 + 5,000 − 3,000 = 50,000; add liabilities.','A balanced equation alone proves every entry is valid.','False; evidence and classification still matter','True; no further review is needed','True when cash is positive','Different errors can offset while the equation still balances.')],
 'debits-credits-and-journal-entries':[
 C('Build a compound entry','One transaction can use more than two accounts. Total debits still equal total credits.','Equipment costing 10,000 with 3,000 cash paid and 7,000 owed: debit equipment 10,000, credit cash 3,000, credit payable 7,000.','Equipment costs 12,000. Cash paid is 4,500 and the rest is financed with a payable. What is the payable credit?',7500,'dollars','12,000 − 4,500.','Why is the payable a credit?','It increases a liability','Every credit reduces cash','It recognizes immediate revenue','The financing obligation is separate from the cash payment and the acquired asset.'),
 C('Reverse an unsupported duplicate','A correction must address the accounts affected by the original error. Reversing a duplicate expense is not new revenue.','A duplicate rent entry debited expense and credited cash; reversal debits cash and credits rent expense.','Rent expense is reported as 6,000 and cash as 9,000. A duplicated 1,200 cash rent entry must be reversed. What is corrected cash?',10200,'dollars','Reverse the duplicate cash credit: 9,000 + 1,200.','What evidence supports the reversal?','The duplicate journal and the single underlying payment','The desire to increase profit','An unexplained balancing amount','Trace the correction to the recorded error and source evidence.')],
 'ledger-and-trial-balance':[
 C('Distinguish balance from accuracy','A wrong account can leave a trial balance balanced. A missing credit instead creates a difference between total columns.','A 500 expense debit posted without its cash credit leaves debits 500 higher.','Debits total 24,500 and credits total 23,700. What is the difference to investigate?',800,'dollars','24,500 − 23,700.','Can you post the difference to an arbitrary account?','No; locate and support the cause','Yes; balance is the only objective','Yes if the difference is small','A suspense or correction process requires actual policy and evidence, not an invented explanation.'),
 C('Separate offsetting errors','A balanced total can conceal errors that offset in aggregate. Reconcile each account to its supporting records.','Cash overstated 400 and receivables understated 400 can leave total assets unchanged.','Two valid cash credits of 600 and 900 were omitted; an unrelated cash debit of 200 was duplicated. Ledger cash is 12,000. What is corrected cash?',10300,'dollars','12,000 − 600 − 900 − 200.','Why examine the source entries individually?','A net difference can hide several distinct causes','Only the largest entry matters','A balanced trial balance rules out errors','Each correction needs its own direction, amount, and support.')],
 'accrual-accounting-and-revenue':[
 C('Separate cash receipts from earned revenue','An advance for undelivered services is a liability in this simplified example. Recognize revenue as the stated service is performed.','A 12,000 advance for 12 equal months initially creates deferred revenue; one month earns 1,000.','A customer prepays 9,000 for 6 equal months of service. Two months have been delivered. What deferred revenue remains?',6000,'dollars','9,000 − 2 × (9,000 / 6).','Does receiving cash establish that all revenue is earned?','No; check the service and contract facts','Yes; cash always equals revenue','Only if the amount is large','Cash timing and service delivery are distinct facts in accrual accounting.'),
 C('Review the period cutoff','Recognize activity in the period supported by the underlying facts. An invoice date alone may not prove when service was delivered.','A December service invoiced in January can create December revenue and a receivable in this simplified scenario.','Reported December revenue is 50,000. It includes 6,000 for January-only services and omits 4,000 of completed December services. What is corrected December revenue?',48000,'dollars','50,000 − 6,000 + 4,000.','What should support the cutoff adjustment?','Delivery evidence and applicable contract terms','Only the desired earnings target','Only when the customer pays','Use the economic facts and relevant recognition rules, not a target result.')],
 'income-statement-and-balance-sheet':[
 C('Connect profit to retained earnings','Net income increases retained earnings in this simplified model. Dividends reduce it and are not an income-statement expense.','Retained earnings 5,000 + income 2,000 − dividends 500 = 6,500.','Opening retained earnings are 12,000. Revenue is 20,000, expenses are 14,000, and dividends are 2,000. What is ending retained earnings?',16000,'dollars','12,000 + (20,000 − 14,000) − 2,000.','Where do dividends belong in this bridge?','As a reduction of retained earnings after income','As sales revenue','As an operating expense here','Owner distributions and costs of earning revenue are different categories.'),
 C('Correct linked statements','A duplicated expense can affect income, assets or liabilities, and equity. Identify the counterpart before correcting.','A duplicate unpaid expense overstates a payable and understates income and equity.','Revenue is 80,000 and expenses are 65,000, including a duplicate unpaid 5,000 invoice. Ignore taxes. What is corrected net income?',20000,'dollars','80,000 − (65,000 − 5,000).','What else changes when the duplicate payable entry is reversed?','Payables fall and equity rises; cash is unchanged','Cash rises by the invoice amount','Revenue rises by the invoice amount','The original entry affected an expense and a payable, not cash or revenue.')],
 'cash-flow-and-reconciliation':[
 C('Bridge income to operating cash','Add back noncash depreciation and adjust for operating working-capital changes. Increasing receivables uses cash relative to income.','Income 500 + depreciation 100 − receivables increase 80 + payables increase 40 = 560.','Net income is 1,200, depreciation 300, receivables rise 400, and operating payables rise 150. What is operating cash flow?',1250,'dollars','1,200 + 300 − 400 + 150.','Why add the increase in operating payables?','Some recognized operating expenses remain unpaid','Payables are always revenue','Borrowing and payables are identical','The bridge adjusts recognized income for operating cash timing.'),
 C('Reconcile all cash movements','A complete cash bridge uses beginning cash and all classified flows. A balanced bridge does not by itself validate classifications.','Beginning cash 1,000 + operating 500 − investing 200 − financing 100 = 1,200.','Beginning cash is 8,000. Income is 3,000, depreciation 1,000, receivables rise 1,500, equipment purchases are 4,000, and new borrowing is 2,000. What is ending cash?',8500,'dollars','8,000 + (3,000 + 1,000 − 1,500) − 4,000 + 2,000.','A purchase was financed entirely without cash. What must the analyst avoid?','Adding a fictional cash receipt and cash payment','Disclosing the noncash transaction separately','Checking the financing documentation','Noncash transactions require appropriate treatment and disclosure, not invented cash flows.')],
 'financial-ratios-and-analysis':[
 C('Compare liquidity measures','A quick ratio excludes inventory in this simplified definition. Asset quality and near-term payment timing still require review.','Cash 20 + receivables 30 divided by current liabilities 25 gives quick ratio 2.0.','Cash is 40, receivables 60, inventory 100, and current liabilities 80. Excluding inventory, what is the quick ratio?',1.25,'times','(40 + 60) / 80.','Why can quick and current ratios differ?','Inventory is excluded from this quick ratio','Current liabilities disappear','Cash is ignored in both','Ratio definitions determine which resources and obligations are compared.'),
 C('Challenge earnings quality','Reported margin can be distorted by a one-time gain. A normalized calculation needs an explicit, supported adjustment.','Reported income 120 including a nonrecurring 40 gain becomes adjusted income 80 before other effects.','Revenue is 1,000 and reported net income is 150, including a 50 one-time gain. Ignore taxes. What adjusted net margin results after removing the gain?',10,'percent','(150 − 50) / 1,000 × 100.','What makes an adjustment defensible?','Transparent evidence and consistent treatment across periods','Removing every unfavorable cost','Using whichever definition looks best','Normalization is a judgment that must remain traceable and consistent.')],
 'three-statement-modeling':[
 C('Link working capital and cash','Higher revenue may require receivables funding. Growing accounting profit does not guarantee growing cash.','Revenue 1,000 with 20% ending receivables means 200 remains uncollected if opening receivables are zero.','Revenue is 5,000, cash operating costs 3,000, depreciation 500, and receivables increase 1,000. Ignore taxes. What is operating cash flow?',1000,'dollars','Income = 5,000 − 3,000 − 500; add depreciation and subtract receivables growth.','Why add depreciation back?','It reduced income without a current cash payment','It is new customer cash','It removes the equipment from all records','The income-to-cash bridge reverses noncash charges before investing flows are considered.'),
 C('Identify a financing need','A forecast can balance while cash falls below an operational minimum. Model the funding need explicitly rather than hiding it in a plug.','Ending cash −200 with a required minimum of 100 indicates 300 of financing need.','Starting cash is 500, operating cash flow 900, capex 1,800, and debt repayment 200. Required minimum cash is 300. What new funding is needed?',900,'dollars','Before funding: 500 + 900 − 1,800 − 200 = −600. Bridge to +300.','How should the funding gap be treated?','Flag a financing assumption and review feasible sources','Force cash to zero without another entry','Increase revenue solely to remove the gap','A financing need is an output to investigate, not evidence that funding is secured.')],
 'month-end-review-and-corrections':[
 C('Post a supported period-end accrual','An unpaid expense belongs to the period in which the relevant service is received in this simplified case. The accrual creates a liability.','Wages earned 2,000 but not paid create expense 2,000 and payable 2,000.','Reported profit is 9,000 before 1,500 of earned but unpaid wages are recorded. Ignore taxes. What is corrected profit?',7500,'dollars','9,000 − 1,500.','Which evidence helps support the accrual?','Approved time records and the relevant pay period','Only the desired month-end profit','Only next month’s sales forecast','An accrual estimates an obligation from supported period facts.'),
 C('Review several adjustments together','Each adjustment has its own sign, period, and supporting evidence. Combine them only after checking their individual effects.','A duplicate expense raises corrected profit when reversed; an omitted expense lowers it when recorded.','Reported profit is 12,000. Reverse a duplicate expense of 800, record omitted wages of 1,200, and defer 2,000 of unearned revenue. Ignore taxes. What is corrected profit?',9600,'dollars','12,000 + 800 − 1,200 − 2,000.','What should the reviewer retain?','The rationale, sources, journal effects, and approval for each adjustment','Only the final net adjustment','A balancing total without period evidence','A net figure can conceal unsupported or offsetting errors.')],
 'financial-statements-for-analysts':[
 C('Align units and margins','Convert reported units before comparing businesses. EBIT margin is EBIT divided by revenue; a larger business need not have a higher margin.','Revenue of 2 billion and EBIT of 300 million gives a 15% margin.','A company reports revenue of 1.5 billion and EBIT of 225 million. What is EBIT margin?',15,'percent','Convert revenue to 1,500 million, then divide 225 by 1,500.','What must be aligned before comparing margins?','Periods, definitions, and units','Only company names','Only share prices','Consistent inputs make the comparison interpretable.'),
 C('Normalize a one-time item','Reported operating profit can contain unusual items. Adjustments need documented evidence and consistent treatment across peers.','EBIT 120 containing a 20 one-time gain gives adjusted EBIT 100.','Revenue is 2,000 million. Reported EBIT is 340 million, including a 60 million one-time gain. What is adjusted EBIT margin?',14,'percent','(340 − 60) / 2,000 × 100.','Should recurring restructuring costs always be excluded?','No; examine recurrence and economic substance','Yes; management labels determine the answer','Yes; higher margins are always more accurate','A normalization policy must withstand evidence and comparison over time.')],
 'enterprise-value-and-equity-value':[
 C('Bridge value to each share','After adjusting enterprise value for financing claims and cash, divide equity value by the relevant share count.','EV 1,000 − debt 300 + cash 100 = equity 800; 40 shares imply 20 per share.','EV is 1,200 million, debt 350 million, cash 50 million, and shares 45 million. What is value per share?',20,'dollars','(1,200 − 350 + 50) / 45.','Why use the appropriate diluted share count?','Other potential equity claims can affect per-share value','EV is always divided by debt','Share count never affects value per share','The bridge and denominator must refer to consistent claims and assumptions.'),
 C('Review additional claims and cash','An EV-to-equity bridge may need preferred claims and noncontrolling interests. Only appropriate cash should be added under the chosen valuation convention.','EV 500 less debt 100 and preferred claims 20 plus excess cash 30 gives equity 410.','EV is 900 million. Debt is 220, preferred claims 30, noncontrolling interest 20, and excess cash 40. All are in millions. What is equity value?',670,'million dollars','900 − 220 − 30 − 20 + 40.','What should you check before adding all cash?','Restrictions and whether the valuation already includes it','Only whether cash is positive','Whether adding more cash improves the answer','Cash treatment must avoid double counting and respect the bridge assumptions.')],
 'forecasting-and-financial-models':[
 C('Separate growth from margin','Revenue growth changes the sales base; margin determines profit from that base. Forecast both explicitly.','Revenue 100 growing 10% becomes 110; a 20% margin produces EBIT 22.','Revenue starts at 800 million, grows 15%, and earns an 18% EBIT margin. What is forecast EBIT?',165.6,'million dollars','800 × 1.15 × 0.18.','Can a growing company have falling profit?','Yes, if costs or margin deteriorate sufficiently','No, growth always raises profit','Only if revenue is zero','Revenue and profitability are separate forecast drivers.'),
 C('Compare a downside operating case','A downside case should change connected assumptions coherently. Lower sales and weaker margins can amplify the effect on profit.','Base revenue 200 at 20% margin gives EBIT 40; downside 180 at 15% gives 27.','Base forecast revenue is 1,000 at a 20% margin. Downside revenue is 10% lower and margin falls to 15%. What is the percentage decline in EBIT?',32.5,'percent','Base EBIT 200; downside 900 × 0.15 = 135. (200 − 135) / 200 × 100.','What makes the downside useful?','Evidence-based connected changes and an explained business mechanism','Reducing every input by the same arbitrary number','Selecting only assumptions that preserve the target value','Stress assumptions should tell a coherent operating story.')],
 'wacc-and-free-cash-flow':[
 C('Weight after-tax financing costs','A simplified WACC weights the required equity return and the after-tax debt cost. Weights add to one.','60% equity at 10% and 40% debt at 5%, with 20% tax, gives 7.6%.','Equity is 70% at 12%; debt is 30% at 6%; the assumed tax rate is 25%. What is WACC?',9.75,'percent','0.70 × 12 + 0.30 × 6 × (1 − 0.25).','Why adjust debt cost here?','The model assumes an applicable interest tax benefit','Debt has no risk','All tax benefits are guaranteed','The adjustment is conditional on the model and applicable tax assumptions.'),
 C('Build unlevered free cash flow','Unlevered FCF starts with after-tax operating profit, adds noncash depreciation, and deducts investment and working-capital needs. Match it with a compatible discount rate.','EBIT 100 at 25% tax, plus depreciation 10, less capex 20 and working capital 5 gives FCF 60.','EBIT is 200, tax 25%, depreciation 30, capex 60, and working capital increases 25. What is unlevered FCF?',95,'million dollars','200 × 0.75 + 30 − 60 − 25.','Which mismatch should be avoided?','Discounting equity-only cash flows at WACC without a consistent framework','Using a cash-flow forecast','Documenting the financing assumptions','The cash-flow claim and discount rate must be conceptually aligned.')],
 'discounted-cash-flow-valuation':[
 C('Discount each period separately','A later cash flow is discounted for more periods. End-of-year timing is the convention in this exercise.','110 in year one and 121 in year two at 10% each have present value 100.','Receive 110 at year one and 121 at year two. At a 10% rate, what is their total present value?',200,'dollars','110 / 1.1 + 121 / 1.1².','Why use a squared denominator in year two?','The cash flow is two periods away','The cash flow is twice as risky by definition','The year-one flow is ignored','Discounting reflects both the rate and time until each modeled cash flow.'),
 C('Discount the terminal value too','A growing perpetuity terminal value is measured at the forecast horizon. Discount it back to today, and require the discount rate to exceed growth.','Year-two FCF 100 with 2% growth and 10% discount rate gives horizon value 102 / 0.08 = 1,275.','Year-one FCF is 90, year-two FCF 100, discount rate 10%, and perpetual growth 2%. What is enterprise value today? Round to two decimals.',1218.18,'dollars','90 / 1.1 + (100 + 100 × 1.02 / (0.10 − 0.02)) / 1.1².','What if perpetual growth approaches the discount rate?','Terminal value becomes extremely sensitive and assumptions need scrutiny','Value becomes reliably precise','The horizon value needs no discounting','A small denominator can dominate the result and magnify uncertainty.')],
 'comparable-companies-and-precedent-transactions':[
 C('Apply a consistent multiple','An EV multiple applied to EBITDA estimates enterprise value, not equity value. Use comparable definitions and periods.','EBITDA 40 at 8× implies EV 320 before the equity bridge.','A peer multiple is 9× EBITDA. Target EBITDA is 50 million, debt is 120 million, and excess cash is 20 million. What is implied equity value?',350,'million dollars','9 × 50 − 120 + 20.','Why is an EV multiple not directly a share-price estimate?','It values operating assets before the financing bridge and share count','It already includes every equity adjustment','It must be multiplied by share price','Keep the numerator and financial measure consistent.'),
 C('Challenge comparability and outliers','A median is less affected by an extreme multiple, but does not solve differences in growth, profitability, accounting, or transaction terms.','For multiples 6×, 7×, 8×, 9×, and 30×, the median is 8×.','Peer multiples are 6×, 8×, 9×, 11×, and 26×. At the median, what EV is implied for EBITDA of 40 million?',360,'million dollars','The middle multiple is 9×. Multiply by 40.','Why review acquisition comparables separately from trading peers?','Control, synergies, timing, and deal terms can differ','Every transaction multiple is automatically superior','Public trading multiples always include the same synergies','Statistical summaries do not remove economic differences.')],
 'mergers-acquisitions-and-capital-raising':[
 C('Measure issuance dilution','New shares increase the total share count. Existing holders own a smaller fraction unless they participate proportionally.','80 existing shares plus 20 new shares leaves old holders with 80% collectively.','A company has 120 million shares and issues 30 million new shares. What percentage do existing holders retain collectively?',80,'percent','120 / (120 + 30) × 100.','Does dilution alone prove the transaction destroys value?','No; assess proceeds, use of capital, price, and resulting value','Yes; a lower ownership percentage always means a lower value','No; every issue automatically creates value','Ownership fraction and economic value are related but distinct.'),
 C('Separate synergies from earnings accretion','A simple combined earnings model can include target earnings, after-tax synergies, financing costs, and new shares. EPS accretion is not the same as value creation.','Buyer earnings 100 plus target 20 less financing cost 10 gives 110 before other adjustments.','Buyer earnings are 200 on 100 shares. Target earnings are 40, after-tax synergies 20, and after-tax financing cost 10. Issue 20 shares. What is combined EPS?',250/120,'dollars per share','(200 + 40 + 20 − 10) / (100 + 20). Round to two decimals.','What else matters even if EPS rises?','Purchase price, risk, integration costs, and returns on invested capital','Only the EPS direction','Only the new company name','An EPS calculation can hide an overpriced or risky acquisition.')],
 'leveraged-buyout-returns':[
 C('Bridge enterprise value to sponsor equity','Entry equity funds the purchase after debt financing. Exit equity is exit EV less remaining debt in this simplified model.','Entry EV 500 with 300 debt uses 200 equity; exit EV 600 less 250 debt yields 350 equity.','Entry EV is 600 with debt 360. Exit EV is 720 and debt remaining is 240. What is MOIC with no interim distributions?',2,'times','Entry equity 240; exit equity 480; divide 480 by 240.','What increases exit equity with unchanged EV?','Supported debt repayment','A larger remaining debt balance','Ignoring the financing claims','Debt repayment reduces claims ahead of sponsor equity.'),
 C('Stress exit value and annualize returns','A lower exit multiple can offset operating growth and debt repayment. Annualizing a two-cash-flow result requires the holding period.','MOIC 1.5 over four years gives 1.5^(1/4) − 1, not 50% each year.','Entry equity is 200. Exit EBITDA is 100, exit multiple 6×, and remaining debt 350. With a five-year hold and no interim flows, what is annualized equity return?',(1.25**0.2-1)*100,'percent','Exit equity = 600 − 350 = 250. (250 / 200)^(1/5) − 1, then × 100. Round to two decimals.','What would require a fuller IRR calculation?','Interim cash flows on their actual dates','A stated holding period alone','A positive MOIC alone','Cash-flow timing changes annualized returns and cannot be ignored.')],
 'pitchbook-and-investment-recommendation':[
 C('Show a valuation range','A recommendation should explain why scenarios differ. A range summarizes model outcomes, not guaranteed transaction prices.','A base value of 100 and downside of 75 imply 25% downside from base.','The base valuation is 800 million and downside valuation is 620 million. What percentage decline does the downside represent?',22.5,'percent','(800 − 620) / 800 × 100.','What should accompany the range?','The assumptions, evidence, and unresolved diligence questions','Only the highest number','A claim that the base case is certain','Decision-makers need to understand the sources of uncertainty.'),
 C('Use scenario weights carefully','A probability-weighted valuation is conditional on estimated probabilities and values. It does not replace downside analysis or diligence.','Values 80 and 120 with equal weights produce a weighted value of 100.','Downside value is 500 at 30%, base value 800 at 50%, and upside value 1,100 at 20%. What is the weighted value?',770,'million dollars','0.30 × 500 + 0.50 × 800 + 0.20 × 1,100.','The weighted value exceeds the offer slightly. What should you do?','Review probability uncertainty, downside, and decision constraints','Describe the difference as guaranteed profit','Delete the downside scenario','Estimated weights can create false precision unless their basis is challenged.')],
 'corporate-actions-and-entitlements':[
 C('Track splits and per-share amounts','A stock split changes eligible share count under the event terms. Adjust per-share figures consistently before computing later distributions.','100 shares in a 2-for-1 split become 200; a subsequent 0.25-per-new-share distribution totals 50.','An eligible holding of 150 shares undergoes a 2-for-1 split, then receives 0.30 per new share. What is gross cash entitlement?',90,'dollars','150 × 2 × 0.30.','What determines which position to use?','The event terms and eligibility record','Whichever position is largest','Only today’s market price','Event ordering and eligibility prevent applying the wrong share basis.'),
 C('Reconcile gross and net entitlements','A stated withholding assumption reduces gross cash. Breaks may arise from eligible position, rate, currency, or adjustments.','Gross cash 1,000 less illustrative 15% withholding gives net 850.','Eligible shares are 2,400 and distribution is 0.75 per share. Apply the scenario’s 15% withholding. Received cash is 1,500. What is expected net cash minus received cash?',30,'dollars','2,400 × 0.75 × 0.85 − 1,500.','A small cash difference is found. What supports closure?','Verify eligibility, event terms, adjustments, and actual receipt','Write it off without evidence','Assume the scenario rate is universal tax law','The withholding rate here is fictional; real treatment requires applicable evidence and rules.')],
 'escalation-and-counterparty-communication':[
 C('Specify the remaining blocker','An escalation should state what remains unresolved after confirmed actions. Avoid counting an expected action as completed.','A 10,000 need with 7,000 confirmed cash leaves 3,000 to resolve.','Cash needed is 18,000. Confirmed cash is 11,000, and another 2,000 has actually arrived. What shortfall remains?',5000,'dollars','18,000 − 11,000 − 2,000.','Should a promised transfer be marked as received?','No; distinguish expected from confirmed cash','Yes if the sender sounds confident','Yes near cutoff','Accurate status keeps the owner and next action clear.'),
 C('Plan a time-sensitive handoff','The latest safe start depends on verification, approval, and transmission time. Use stated times and preserve required controls.','A 16:00 deadline with 20 minutes of work remaining means the latest start is 15:40.','A fictional cutoff is 16:00. Verification takes 15 minutes, independent review 10, and transmission 5. It is 15:20. How many minutes of buffer remain?',10,'minutes','40 minutes until cutoff minus 30 minutes of required steps.','What if the required sequence no longer fits?','Escalate the risk and approved alternatives while retaining controls','Skip independent review automatically','Mark settlement complete before transmission','A missed process window needs an honest handoff, not an unsupported status change.')],
 'controls-regulatory-awareness-and-incident-review':[
 C('Measure exceptions with a denominator','A count of exceptions is more useful with its population and period. A falling count can still mean a rising rate if activity falls faster.','10 exceptions in 200 records is 5%; 8 in 100 is 8%.','A desk processes 250 records and finds 15 exceptions. What is the exception rate?',6,'percent','15 / 250 × 100.','What should accompany a rate comparison?','Consistent population, definitions, and periods','Only the total count','A claim that every exception is a realized loss','A metric supports investigation when its meaning and denominator are clear.'),
 C('Distinguish remediation from control effectiveness','Fixing individual records is not proof that the underlying control now works. Review recurrence and supported test evidence.','A follow-up test finding 3 failures in 100 checks has a 3% observed failure rate.','A follow-up sample of 160 records contains 8 repeat control failures. What is the observed failure rate?',5,'percent','8 / 160 × 100.','Can the control be declared effective just because the affected records were corrected?','No; evaluate root cause, design, and operating evidence','Yes; correction proves prevention','Yes if no one complains','Remediation and assurance address different questions, and sample limits still apply.')],
 'operations-markets':[
 C('Compare instrument units','Equities use shares times price. A bond quoted per 100 of face value needs a different conversion.','50,000 face at 98 has a clean quoted value of 49,000 before other items.','A fictional bond has 80,000 face value and a quote of 97.5 per 100. What is its clean quoted value?',78000,'dollars','80,000 × 97.5 / 100.','Why check the quantity unit?','Face value and share quantity require different calculations','Every quote is dollars per share','Bond face value is always profit','Instrument conventions determine how a quote converts to value.'),
 C('Separate gross activity from net exposure','Gross trade value and a net cash difference describe different things. Neither alone is a realized loss or a complete risk measure.','Buying 10,000 and selling 8,000 produces 18,000 gross activity and a 2,000 net purchase before other items.','Buy 500 shares at 40 and sell 300 at 42. What is gross traded value?',32600,'dollars','500 × 40 + 300 × 42.','Does the gross amount measure profit?','No; profit needs cost basis, positions, and other relevant facts','Yes; all traded value is earned income','Yes if both trades settle','Use each measure only for the question it actually answers.')],
 'operations-lifecycle':[
 C('Separate processing milestones','Booked, matched, ready, and settled describe different milestones. A later label requires evidence of the relevant step.','Of 20 booked trades, 15 matched and 9 settled means 6 matched trades still await settlement.','A batch has 40 booked trades, 32 matched, and 25 settled. All settled trades are matched. How many matched trades remain unsettled?',7,'trades','32 − 25.','Should all matched trades be marked settled?','No; confirm completed cash and securities movement','Yes; the labels mean the same thing','Only if the batch is small','Agreement about terms is distinct from delivery.'),
 C('Reconcile a batch without double counting','Nested status counts are not separate populations. Break the batch into mutually exclusive groups before adding totals.','100 booked, 80 matched, 60 settled means 20 unmatched, 20 matched-unsettled, and 60 settled.','A batch contains 120 booked trades, 95 matched, and 70 settled. Settled trades are included in matched. How many are not yet settled in total?',50,'trades','120 − 70; do not add the nested counts.','What makes a batch summary reliable?','Clear status definitions and reconciled, non-overlapping groups','Adding every displayed counter together','Excluding trades with missing status','Control totals depend on how categories overlap.')],
 'operations-settlement':[
 C('Combine required cash with available funds','Calculate the full stated settlement obligation, including scenario charges, and compare it with confirmed available funds.','100 shares at 20 plus a 10 charge requires 2,010.','Buy 120 shares at 50 and pay a stated 15 charge. Confirmed cash is 5,700. What cash shortfall remains?',315,'dollars','120 × 50 + 15 − 5,700.','What does a matched ticket prove?','The relevant details agree, not that funding exists','Cash is guaranteed to arrive','Delivery has already completed','Funding and settlement evidence remain separate readiness checks.'),
 C('Avoid unsupported netting assumptions','Cash offsets can be used only when the scenario and actual arrangements support their timing and availability. Expected proceeds are not automatically available cash.','An unconfirmed sale receipt should not silently fund a confirmed purchase obligation.','Two purchases require 8,000 and 5,000. Confirmed cash is 9,000; another 3,000 of sale proceeds is expected but unconfirmed. What is the confirmed funding shortfall?',4000,'dollars','8,000 + 5,000 − 9,000.','How should expected sale proceeds be handled?','Track separately until their availability is confirmed','Treat them as settled cash automatically','Remove the purchase obligations','A funding plan distinguishes expected flows from confirmed resources.')],
 'operations-matching':[
 C('Quantify a price discrepancy','Equal quantities can still conceal a price break. Calculate its cash impact without assuming which source is correct.','100 shares at 50 versus 50.10 differ by 10 in cash value.','Both records show 400 shares. One price is 25.10 and the other 25.25. What is the absolute cash difference?',60,'dollars','400 × (25.25 − 25.10).','What determines the correct price?','Verified execution evidence','The higher value','An average of the two prices','A difference identifies a question; approved evidence supports its resolution.'),
 C('Check offsetting field errors','Two trades can have similar notionals while differing in quantity and price. Compare fields rather than using one aggregate total.','200 at 50 and 250 at 40 both equal 10,000 but describe different quantities and prices.','One record shows 300 shares at 40; another shows 240 at 50. What is the absolute quantity difference?',60,'shares','300 − 240.','Equal notionals mean the trade is matched.','False; instrument, quantity, price, account, and dates still matter','True; notional is the only field','True if both records are positive','Offsetting field errors can disappear in an aggregate check.')],
 'operations-reconciliation':[
 C('Explain a supported timing difference','A timing item can reconcile records when supported by delivery and posting evidence. It does not authorize changing either record arbitrarily.','Internal 1,050 and custody 1,000 may reconcile if 50 newly delivered shares await custody posting.','Internal holdings are 1,250 shares and custody shows 1,100. A verified 100-share delivery awaits custody posting. What unexplained difference remains?',50,'shares','1,250 − 1,100 − 100.','Does a partial explanation close the entire break?','No; investigate the remaining difference','Yes if most of it is explained','Yes if the original amount was small','Supported timing explains only the amount it actually accounts for.'),
 C('Reconcile movements as well as balances','Expected closing holdings equal opening holdings plus supported receipts minus deliveries, adjusted for verified events. Check signs and timestamps.','Opening 500 + receipts 100 − deliveries 40 = expected closing 560.','Opening holdings are 2,000. Verified receipts are 350 and deliveries 180. External closing holdings are 2,120. What is expected closing minus external closing?',50,'shares','2,000 + 350 − 180 − 2,120.','A proposed adjustment makes totals agree but has no evidence. What next?','Retain the break and investigate','Post it to improve the completion rate','Delete the opening balance','Reconciliation closes with an explained outcome, not merely equal totals.')],
 'operations-exceptions':[
 C('Track the remaining queue honestly','New breaks, resolved breaks, and reopened breaks change the queue. A release decision needs its own evidence.','Start with 20 breaks, resolve 8, add 3: 15 remain open.','A queue starts with 35 breaks. Resolve 12, receive 6 new breaks, and reopen 2 previously closed items. How many are open?',31,'breaks','35 − 12 + 6 + 2.','Does clearing a break guarantee settlement?','No; track the downstream delivery outcome','Yes; all stages are equivalent','Only for high-value trades','A correction and an actual completed settlement are distinct events.'),
 C('Prioritize consequence and deadline','A high-value later trade may be less urgent than a smaller due-now trade with an unresolved control issue. Document the basis for triage.','A queue’s gross value helps describe scope, but does not by itself identify the next action.','Three open breaks have notionals of 40,000, 25,000, and 15,000. The 25,000 break is resolved with evidence. What gross exception notional remains?',55000,'dollars','40,000 + 15,000.','How should the next break be chosen?','Consider deadline, impact, uncertainty, and ownership together','Always choose the largest number','Always choose the oldest screen row','A single ranking metric cannot represent every operational consequence.')],
 'operations-controls':[
 C('Count independent reviews','A completed change and its required independent approval are separate checkpoints. Self-approval does not satisfy the scenario control.','10 changes with 7 valid independent reviews leave 3 awaiting review.','There are 18 instruction changes. Twelve have independent approval and two were self-approved. How many still lack valid independent approval?',6,'changes','18 − 12. Self-approvals are not valid independent checks here.','What should happen to an unverified change near cutoff?','Hold release and escalate through approved channels','Skip the review to save time','Treat the sender’s name as verification','The illustrative control applies under time pressure too.'),
 C('Test control coverage','A high pass rate is meaningful only with a defined population and reliable evidence. Review what the untested population might contain.','Testing 80 of 100 required reviews leaves 20 outside the observed test coverage.','A population has 200 changes. Review evidence was tested for 150; 12 of those lacked a valid approval. What percent of the tested sample failed?',8,'percent','12 / 150 × 100.','Can this prove exactly 8% of the whole population failed?','No; sample selection and uncertainty matter','Yes; every sample exactly represents its population','Yes if the calculation is correct','A sample result is evidence with limits, not a complete census.')],
 'operations-cutoff':[
 C('Compare workload with capacity','Capacity estimates count work that can actually be processed within the available time. Unresolved blockers still need action.','At 4 clean tickets per minute for 10 minutes, modeled capacity is 40.','There are 45 clean tickets and 8 minutes left. Processing capacity is 5 tickets per minute. How many exceed modeled capacity?',5,'tickets','45 − 8 × 5.','Does adding staff automatically resolve missing funding?','No; funding needs its own supported action','Yes; every blocker is a capacity issue','Only if the queue is large','Processing throughput and readiness blockers are different constraints.'),
 C('Budget time for rework and handoff','A realistic deadline plan reserves time for exceptions and handoff. Separate a modeled processing limit from confirmed completion.','A 30-minute window with 10 minutes reserved leaves 20 for processing.','A fictional desk has 25 minutes left, reserves 5 for handoff, and processes 3 clean tickets per minute. With 72 clean tickets waiting, how many exceed planned capacity?',12,'tickets','72 − (25 − 5) × 3.','What is a useful action when capacity is insufficient?','Escalate the quantified gap, priorities, owners, and approved alternatives','Mark all tickets complete','Remove the handoff record','A transparent plan preserves control and communicates unresolved work.')],
 'specialist-credit':[
 C('Estimate a modeled credit loss','A simplified expected loss multiplies exposure by default probability and loss given default. This is an expectation, not a certain loss.','Exposure 100,000 × 2% probability × 40% severity gives expected loss 800.','Exposure is 250,000, default probability 3%, and loss given default 40%. What is expected loss?',3000,'dollars','250,000 × 0.03 × 0.40.','Does expected loss establish a borrower’s creditworthiness?','No; review wider evidence and limitations','Yes; one formula is sufficient','Yes if the result is small','A model output supports, but does not replace, a documented credit review.'),
 C('Stress cash and debt service together','Coverage can weaken because available cash falls, required payments rise, or both. Compare the downside with the base using consistent periods.','60,000 cash / 40,000 service = 1.5×; a 20% cash decline gives 1.2× at unchanged service.','Base annual cash is 90,000 and debt service 60,000. Cash falls 20% and debt service rises 10%. What is stressed DSCR?',72/66,'times','90,000 × 0.8 / (60,000 × 1.1). Round to two decimals.','What does coverage near 1× suggest?','Limited modeled cushion; investigate liquidity and assumptions','Automatic approval','A guaranteed default date','Coverage must be interpreted with cash quality, timing, and other evidence.')],
 'specialist-venture':[
 C('Connect receipts, costs, and runway','Net burn is cash costs minus cash receipts. Runway assumes that burn remains constant and ignores future financing unless stated.','Cash 300,000 with 80,000 costs and 30,000 receipts gives 6 months.','Cash is 480,000. Monthly costs are 120,000 and receipts 40,000. What is modeled runway?',6,'months','480,000 / (120,000 − 40,000).','Can a promised funding round be treated as cash already received?','No; keep unconfirmed financing separate','Yes if the valuation is attractive','Yes if revenue grows','Runway is based on the stated cash and assumptions, not a funding promise.'),
 C('Model staged burn','A changing cash burn requires a period-by-period bridge. A single constant-burn ratio can hide a near-term step-up.','Cash 300,000 with two months at 50,000 burn leaves 200,000; then 100,000 burn lasts two more months.','Cash is 600,000. Burn is 80,000 per month for three months, then 120,000 per month. No new funding arrives. How many total months until cash reaches zero?',6,'months','After three months: 600,000 − 240,000 = 360,000. That funds three further months.','What should accompany a fundraising recommendation?','Milestones, financing uncertainty, burn sensitivity, and a contingency plan','Only the largest market-size estimate','A guaranteed next-round date','Runway supports planning under assumptions, not certainty about funding.')],
 'specialist-property':[
 C('Translate vacancy into operating income','Vacancy reduces rental receipts before operating expenses in this simplified model. Debt service is reviewed separately.','Potential rent 500,000 at 10% vacancy less 150,000 costs gives NOI 300,000.','Potential rent is 600,000, vacancy 15%, and operating costs 210,000. What is NOI?',300000,'dollars','600,000 × 0.85 − 210,000.','Why exclude debt service from this NOI calculation?','NOI measures property operations before financing','Debt service is never paid','Vacancy pays the loan automatically','Financing is assessed separately, including coverage of the required payments.'),
 C('Stress income and capitalization rate','A lower NOI and higher cap rate both reduce an income-capitalized value. This is one simplified valuation approach.','NOI 300,000 at 6% implies 5 million; at 7.5% it implies 4 million.','Base NOI is 400,000 with a 5% cap rate. Stress NOI down 10% and cap rate to 6%. What percentage decline in indicated value results?',25,'percent','Base 400,000 / 0.05 = 8 million; stress 360,000 / 0.06 = 6 million.','Why review leases and capital expenditure too?','The one-year stabilized model omits timing and reinvestment needs','The cap rate guarantees every future receipt','Only the property name affects value','A simple capitalization formula does not describe all property cash-flow risks.')],
 'specialist-planning':[
 C('Calculate a contribution gap','At a stated zero return, savings growth comes only from contributions. Separate an affordable contribution from an assumed market return.','A 5,000 gap over 10 months requires 500 each month at zero return.','A fictional goal is 10,000 in 20 months. Starting savings are 4,000 and return is zero. What monthly contribution is required?',300,'dollars','(10,000 − 4,000) / 20.','If the contribution is unaffordable, what should be reviewed?','Goal, timing, budget, and uncertainty','Promise a higher return','Ignore the person’s constraints','Planning involves tradeoffs, not guaranteed investment outcomes.'),
 C('Separate nominal and real purchasing power','Inflation reduces what a future nominal balance can buy. Real value discounts by the assumed price-level growth.','121 next year with 10% inflation has current purchasing power 110.','A future balance is 121,000 after two years. Assume inflation of 10% each year. What is its value in today’s purchasing power?',100000,'dollars','121,000 / 1.1².','Does a nominal savings target fully describe future affordability?','No; inflation and the price of the intended goal matter','Yes; money always buys the same amount','Only if the balance is positive','Nominal balances and real purchasing power answer different questions.')],
 'specialist-insurance':[
 C('Add expenses to expected claims','A combined ratio compares claims plus underwriting expenses with premium in this simplified one-year exercise. It excludes other effects.','Claims 500,000 plus expenses 180,000 on premium 800,000 gives 85%.','Expected claims are 600,000, expenses 200,000, and premium 1,000,000. What is the combined ratio?',80,'percent','(600,000 + 200,000) / 1,000,000 × 100.','Does a ratio below 100% guarantee total company profitability?','No; actual claims and other financial effects may differ','Yes; expectations are certain','Yes; no other costs or risks exist','This limited expectation is not a full insurer financial result.'),
 C('Stress frequency and severity','Claim frequency and severity can worsen together. Multiplying both changes can exceed the effect of either change alone.','A 20% frequency increase and 10% severity increase multiply baseline claims by 1.32.','Base expected claims are 500,000. Frequency rises 20% and severity 25%. Expenses remain 150,000 and premium 900,000. What is the stressed combined ratio?',100,'percent','500,000 × 1.2 × 1.25 = 750,000; add 150,000 and divide by 900,000.','What else may be missing from this stress?','Dependence, extreme events, reserves, and reinsurance terms','Every possible insurance risk is captured','A guaranteed estimate of actual claims','A small scenario set cannot represent every tail or timing risk.')],
 'specialist-budget':[
 C('Split price and volume effects','Use budget price for the volume effect and actual units for the price effect in this bridge. The two effects sum to the revenue variance.','Budget 100 at 20; actual 90 at 22. Volume is −200, price +180, net −20.','Budget is 1,000 units at 30; actual is 900 at 32. What is the signed price effect?',1800,'dollars','900 × (32 − 30).','Why check that the bridge sums to total variance?','It tests internal consistency of the chosen decomposition','It proves the business cause without investigation','It replaces all source records','A correct decomposition still needs evidence about what drove the changes.'),
 C('Separate contribution from profit','Contribution subtracts variable costs, while operating profit also reflects fixed costs in this simplified example. Higher revenue need not improve either margin.','Revenue 100,000 less variable costs 60,000 and fixed costs 25,000 gives profit 15,000.','Actual sales are 2,000 units at 40 each. Variable cost is 26 per unit and fixed costs are 20,000. What is operating profit?',8000,'dollars','2,000 × (40 − 26) − 20,000.','Why inspect a favorable revenue variance alongside costs?','Pricing, volume, and cost changes can have different profit effects','Revenue is always profit','Costs do not matter when sales rise','A useful performance explanation connects revenue drivers with cost behavior.')],
 'specialist-controls':[
 C('Distinguish inherent and residual scores','The training score multiplies impact by likelihood, then applies an assumed control reduction. These ordinal scores are not money or a measured loss probability.','Impact 4 × likelihood 3 = 12; a 50% reduction leaves an illustrative residual score of 6.','Impact is 5, likelihood 4, and assumed control effectiveness 60%. What is the illustrative residual score?',8,'score','5 × 4 × (1 − 0.60).','What supports the control-effectiveness assumption?','Design review and operating evidence','The desired residual score','Only a manager’s confidence','The assumed reduction must be challenged; arithmetic alone does not validate it.'),
 C('Compare a weakening-control scenario','An assumed reduction can deteriorate when a control is bypassed or fails. Compare scenarios without calling an ordinal score a financial loss.','Inherent score 20 reduced 75% leaves 5; reduced only 25% leaves 15.','An inherent score is 16. Effectiveness falls from 75% to 25%. By how many score points does the illustrative residual score rise?',8,'score points','Old 16 × 0.25 = 4; new 16 × 0.75 = 12; difference 8.','What should follow evidence that a control failed?','Contain the issue, retain evidence, assign ownership, and reassess','Only change the dashboard color','Treat the score increase as an actual cash loss','An operational response must connect the evidence to supported actions and follow-up.')],
};
